<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Iwellemmeden Kel Dinnik</h1><p class="st-meta">1770 CE – 1917 CE (approx.) · capital Tahoua</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Iwellemmeden Tuareg confederation around Tahoua, a power on the northern frontier of Hausaland and the Sokoto Caliphate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwellemmeden">Iwellemmeden</a> confederation split in the 18th century, around 1770, into a western branch, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/iwellemmeden-kel-ataram">Iwellemmeden Kel Ataram</a>, and an eastern branch, the Kel Dinnik. The Kel Dinnik settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azawagh">Azawagh</a> and the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahoua">Tahoua</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Led by an amenokal from a noble lineage, the Kel Dinnik ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> herding groups and dependent farming villages in the Ader region. They raided and traded with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gobir">Kingdom of Gobir</a>, and in the 19th century alternately fought and cooperated with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. Salt, livestock and grain were the basis of the economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahoua">Tahoua</a> and the surrounding region around 1900. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> uprisings during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> were crushed by 1917, and the confederation was integrated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a> under French administration.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/iwellemmeden-kel-dinnik.jpg" alt="Iwellemmeden Kel Dinnik" width="320" height="420"><figcaption>Image: Felix Dubois (author of book) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niqab_on_Tuareg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1770 CE – 1917 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tahoua</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamajaq (Tuareg)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/niger">Niger</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eastern Iwellemmeden, Kel Dinnik</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=iwellemmeden-kel-dinnik" aria-label="Iwellemmeden Kel Dinnik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 1624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Iwellemmeden Kel Dinnik lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5144 1880L4523 1888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4523 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5144 1880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5144 1880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tahoua. <a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=iwellemmeden-kel-dinnik">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
